==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|mahhōn|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*makōną}}; akin to Low German {{term|maken|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|maken|lang=nl}}, English {{term|make|lang=en}}, West Frisian {{term|meitsje|lang=fy}}. Ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*mag-||to knead, mix, make}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmaxən/|[ˈmaχən]|[ˈmaχn̩]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-machen.ogg|audio (Germany)|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-machen.ogg|audio (Austria and Southern Germany)|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|aχn|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|macht|machte|gemacht}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[make]]; to [[produce]]; to [[create]] an object, arrangement, situation etc.
#: ''Ich '''hab''' dir einen Kuchen '''gemacht'''!'' — I '''have made''' you a pie!
#: ''Du hast einen Fehler '''gemacht'''.'' - You '''made''' a mistake.
# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[take]] (a [[photo]])
# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[prepare]] {{qualifier|e.g. food, drinks}}
#: '''''Machst''' du heute das Essen?'' — '''Will''' you '''prepare''' dinner today?
#: ''sich eine Pizza '''machen''''' — to heat up or make a pizza for oneself
# {{context|transitive|informal|lang=de}} to [[do]] {{qualifier|to perform an action}}
#: '''''Mach''' es!'' — '''Do''' it!
#: ''Das '''hat''' er ganz allein '''gemacht'''!'' — He '''has done''' that all by himself!
# {{context|transitive|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[matter]] (only [[impersonally]])
#: ''Das '''macht''' nichts!'' — That doesn't '''matter'''!
# {{context|transitive|informal|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[come to]]; to [[total]]; to [[cost]] {{qualifier|prizes}}
#: ''Wie viel '''macht''' das?'' — How much '''does''' that '''come to'''?
# {{context|transitive|informal|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[earn]]; to receive or create [[profit]]
#: ''Der Herr Müller ist echt reich; der '''macht''' mehr als 5000 im Monat.'' — Mr Müller is quite rich; he '''earns''' more than 5000 bucks per month.

====Usage notes====
Unlike English, the verb {{term|machen|lang=de}} ({{term|make|lang=en}}) is used as a synonym for {{term|tun|lang=de}} ({{term|do|lang=en}}) in most cases. However, [[tun]] can not be used for the proper senses of ''machen''.

